1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,200
So far we've been writing assembly. But what the hardware actually runs is a sequence of 32-bit binary numbers.

2
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:19,800
Instructions are data too, kept in memory just like any other data: that's the stored-program idea.

3
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:23,566
The PC holds the memory address of the current instruction.

4
00:00:23,566 --> 00:00:28,366
So how exactly does a line of assembly become 32 bits?

5
00:00:28,366 --> 00:00:37,766
The 32 bits are cut into pieces called fields. Instructions whose operands are all registers, like add and sub, use the R-type format.

6
00:00:37,766 --> 00:00:47,333
rd, rs1 and rs2 get 5 bits each: 2 to the 5th is 32, just enough to number all 32 registers.

7
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:56,233
The opcode, in the lowest 7 bits, says what kind of instruction this is; funct3 and funct7 pin down the exact operation.

8
00:00:56,233 --> 00:01:00,533
Let's encode add x18, x19, x10.

9
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:13,466
rd = 18 is 10010 in 5-bit binary; rs1 = 19 is 10011; rs2 = 10 is 01010.

10
00:01:13,466 --> 00:01:22,266
For add, funct3 and funct7 are both 0, and the opcode for R-type arithmetic is 0110011.

11
00:01:22,266 --> 00:01:29,800
Every 4 bits make one hex digit: the whole instruction is 0x00A98933.

12
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:40,733
What about sub? Only funct7 changes, to 0100000. That 1 in bit 30 tells the hardware to make the adder subtract.

13
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:48,966
All R-type operations share one opcode; the combination of funct3 and funct7 tells them apart.

14
00:01:48,966 --> 00:01:53,866
addi has only two registers, plus a constant. Where does the constant go?

15
00:01:53,866 --> 00:02:02,800
I-type merges R-type's funct7 and rs2 slots into one 12-bit immediate; every other field stays exactly where it was.

16
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:14,866
That answers the question from Episode 2: the immediate only gets these 12 bits, and 12-bit two's complement spans exactly −2048 to 2047.

17
00:02:14,866 --> 00:02:26,866
Encode addi x15, x1, -50: -50 in 12-bit two's complement is 1111 1100 1110.

18
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:38,900
When it runs, the hardware sign-extends these 12 bits to 32 and adds them to rs1. The encoding is 0xFCE08793.

19
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:49,433
Loads are I-type too. In lw x14, 8(x2), the offset 8 is the immediate and the base register x2 goes in rs1.

20
00:02:49,433 --> 00:02:59,266
All loads share one opcode; funct3 picks the width and signedness: lb, lh, lw, lbu, lhu.

21
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:10,933
slli, srli and srai are I-type too, but a shift amount is at most 31, so only the immediate's low 5 bits are used.

22
00:03:10,933 --> 00:03:18,266
A store has two source registers, the data and the base, and no destination, but it still needs a 12-bit offset.

23
00:03:18,266 --> 00:03:26,733
S-type's trick: split the immediate in two. The upper 7 bits go where funct7 was, the lower 5 where rd used to be.

24
00:03:26,733 --> 00:03:42,566
Encode sw x14, 8(x2). The offset 8 in 12 bits is 0000000 01000: the upper 7 bits are all 0, the lower 5 are 01000.

25
00:03:42,566 --> 00:03:55,266
The data to store, x14, goes in rs2 and the base x2 in rs1; funct3 = 010 means a full word, and the opcode is 0100011.

26
00:03:55,266 --> 00:04:02,900
Put together, this sw is 0x00E12423.

27
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:08,133
Stack the three formats: why does S-type split its immediate so awkwardly?

28
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:14,533
The answer: in every format that uses them, rs1 and rs2 sit in the same place.

29
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:22,900
The same goes for funct3 and opcode. The hardware can read the registers from fixed positions before it even knows the instruction type.

30
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:31,833
Register reads and decoding can happen at the same time: simpler, faster circuits. A lovely touch in RISC-V's design.

